# Spec: location based note taking

## initial idea

I'm going to travel, and to prepare i've been taking notes I however have loads of them now and it isn't practical to just have them in a list.
Rather it would make more sense to have them in a hierarchy based on location. 
So Japan --> Kanto/Tokyo --> Ginza | Akihabara | Shinjuku
and further we could go:

Tokyo --> asakusa --> senoji | kappabashi | kagetsudou Melon Pan store | Don Quijote Asakusa

Now we could have a map Which shows to level notes on it as pins e.g. Japan,
Then select that top level, this then shows the sub levels e.g. Kanto/Tokyo , Kansai etc
selecting one may also show the notes attached to it while still showing the map.


## User stories

### Location hierarchy
- As a traveler, I want my notes tied to a location, so that I can easily find them based on their physical location.
- As a note taker, I want my locations ordered in a strict tree (each location has exactly one parent, a parent can have many children), so that navigation is unambiguous.
- As a note taker, I want my locations ordered in a hierarchy (e.g. Country → Region → City → District/Place), so that I can find notes by drilling down from broad to specific.
  - This is order is arbitrary (sometimes maybe just 1 layer, somtimes 3, 5 , 20 etc)
- As a note taker, I want to create a new location node under an existing parent by pressing on the map in "add mode", so that I can extend the hierarchy as I discover new places to note down.
- As a note taker, I want to rename or edit a location (incl. moving its pin in "edit mode"), so that I can fix typos or clarify a place name/position without losing its notes or children.
- As a note taker, I want to archive a location instead of deleting it outright, so that I can hide places I no longer need without losing history.
  - Archived locations stay visible in the list view (greyed out) but are hidden from the map.
  - A location can only be permanently deleted once it is archived.
  - A location cannot be archived or deleted while it still has non-archived children — all children must be archived/deleted first.
- As a note taker, I want to move a location (and its children) under a different parent, so that I can reorganize my hierarchy as it grows.
- As a note taker, I want to give two different locations the same name under different parents (e.g. two "Ginza"), so that I don't have to invent artificial disambiguation.

### Notes
- As a note taker, I want to attach a note to any level of the hierarchy (not just the most specific one), so that I can jot down general notes about a country/region as well as a specific shop.
- As a note taker, I want to move a note from one location to another, so that I can correct or refine where it belongs.
- As a note taker, I want to see all notes for a location including or excluding its children, so that I can choose between a focused view and a full regional overview.
- As a note taker, I want to create/edit/delete notes the same way I do today, but with an added location field, so that existing note-taking workflows still work.

### Map interaction
- As a traveler, I want to see top-level locations (e.g. countries) as pins on a map, so that I get an overview of everywhere I have notes.
- As a traveler, I want to select a pin to zoom into its child locations, so that I can drill down the hierarchy visually instead of through a list.
- As a traveler, I want to select a location and see its attached notes alongside the map, so that I can read notes without losing map context.
- As a traveler, I want to navigate back up to a parent level from the map, so that I can move between regions easily.
- As a note taker, I want to add a new location by pressing on the map while in "add mode", so that the location's coordinates are captured directly instead of typed in.
- As a note taker, I want a list/tree view of locations in addition to the map, so that I can still browse and manage archived locations that no longer appear on the map.

### Search & navigation
- As a traveler, I want to search notes and locations by name, so that I can jump straight to a place without navigating the whole hierarchy.
- As a traveler, I want breadcrumbs showing the current path in the hierarchy (e.g. Japan > Kanto > Tokyo), so that I always know where I am.

## Concerns not placed under a user story
- Notes will likely gain their own `latitude`/`longitude` fields (independent of the location's coordinates), since a note is tied to a location but may refer to a precise spot within it. This is an implementation/data-model detail to work out during planning rather than a user story.

## Resolved decisions
- **Hierarchy shape**: strictly a tree — one parent per location, a parent may have many children, arbitrary depth.
- **Deletion**: no hard delete of "live" locations. Locations must be archived first (hidden from map, greyed out in list), and a location can only be archived/deleted once it has no non-archived children. Deletion is only possible after archiving.
- **Location creation**: done by pressing on the map in "add mode" (and repositioning in "edit mode"), so coordinates come from the map rather than manual entry. Naming stays free text.
- **Duplicate names**: allowed — the same name can exist under different parents; identity is by hierarchy position/id, not name.
- **Multi-tenancy**: single-user for now; sharing/multi-user is explicitly out of scope until revisited.
